title: Decide whether a man page is worth generating
effort: S
area: docs
created: 2026-09-17T06:46:56Z
updated: 2026-09-18T20:38:27Z
handoff:

Debian's guidelines want a man page for anything in bin. A Homebrew tap and
the AUR do not care, and those are the routes 29 does first.

The usage text already carries the command list and the environment
variables, so a man page would be generated from the same function rather
than written and kept in step by hand — the copies-go-stale rule applies,
and check already compares the two generated documents against what would
be generated now.

Decide: generate one from usage and let check compare it, or ship none and
accept that a distro-native package is further away than it looks.

Done when the decision is written down, and implemented if it is yes.


Decided 2026-09-18, and implemented.

Generate one. The argument against was never the page, it was the second
copy: a hand-written pidly.1 is a command list that drifts from the one the
CLI prints, and the format has refused that everywhere else. Generating it
from the usage text removes the objection rather than answering it, so the
Debian route stays open at the cost of no maintenance at all.

usage() was split into usage_text, which writes to stdout, and usage(), which
sends it to stderr and exits 2. The page is built from usage_text, so the
COMMANDS and ENVIRONMENT sections cannot disagree with pidly help — there is
only one list. The prose sections (DESCRIPTION, FILES, EXIT STATUS, SEE ALSO)
are written, because they describe the format rather than the command list
and nothing else holds them.

Two things the generation has to get right:

- Roff reads a backslash as markup and a leading . or ' as a control line, so
  usage text is passed through roff_escape on its way in. It matches the
  em-dash by its bytes rather than as a literal, so the escaping does not
  depend on this script's encoding surviving a copy. Same class of fault
  as 51: a character crossing a layer that quietly reinterprets it.
- Nothing in the page is dated or versioned. A .TH carrying today's date
  would differ from yesterday's committed copy every morning, so check would
  report drift that is the clock rather than the content, and the fix would
  be to regenerate daily forever. The date field is empty on purpose.

check compares a shipped page like it compares the other generated documents,
and only if one exists: docs/pidly.1, man/pidly.1 or pidly.1. The page
documents pidly rather than the project's tracker, so a project that never
generated one is not missing anything and is not nagged. pidly's own
repository is the one that wants it checked, and docs/pidly.1 is committed.

Guarded in man.sh: every command in the usage text reaches the page, no raw
non-ASCII survives, no line inside a .nf block opens with a bare dot, groff
renders it without a warning, two runs are byte-identical, and check reports
a stale page with the command that fixes it and says nothing when there is
none. portable.sh runs man under every shell it covers.
